---
import ZetaLayoutV2 from "../../../sites/zeta/ZetaLayoutV2.astro";
---

<ZetaLayoutV2
	lang="en"
	title="Zeta — The Agentic Development Environment"
	description="Zeta is an ADE (Agentic Development Environment) with three entries: zetacode (agent CLI and TUI), zetaide (terminal IDE and ACP client), and Work Studio — the workbench in your browser and as a self-contained desktop app. Linux-first, Windows supported."
	active="home"
>
	<section class="hero">
		<div class="container hero-grid">
			<div class="hero-copy">
				<div class="kicker">Agentic Development Environment · by Linxira-OS</div>
				<h1>One workbench.<br><span class="accent">Three ways in.</span></h1>
				<p class="lede">
					<strong>Zeta</strong> is an <strong>ADE</strong> (Agentic Development Environment): the session, not the
					file, is the unit of work. Three entries share one runtime — <strong>zetacode</strong> (agent CLI and
					TUI), <strong>zetaide</strong> (terminal IDE, and an ACP client for external agents) and
					<strong>Work Studio</strong>, the workbench in your browser or as a self-contained desktop app.
					Linux is the primary platform; Windows is supported.
				</p>
				<div class="cta-row">
					<a class="cta primary" href="#install">Get started</a>
					<a class="cta ghost" href="/zeta/en/features/">See features →</a>
					<a class="cta ghost" href="https://github.com/Linxira-OS/linxira-zeta/releases/latest" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Desktop app</a>
				</div>
			</div>

			<!-- Pure-CSS terminal mock: a zetacode session and the Work Studio hand-off -->
			<div class="term" role="img" aria-label="Mock of a zetacode session that starts Work Studio">
				<div class="term-bar">
					<span class="dot"></span><span class="dot"></span><span class="dot"></span>
					<span class="title">zetacode — ~/projects/linxira</span>
				</div>
				<div class="pane">
					<div class="pane-h on"><span class="ph-t">● zetacode</span><span class="ctl">session · linxira</span></div>
					<div class="out">
						<div><span class="prompt">❯</span> zetacode</div>
						<div><b>▸ planning</b> refactor: workbench layout</div>
						<div><span class="ok">✓</span> 3 files patched, tests green</div>
						<div><span class="prompt">❯</span> zetacode serve</div>
						<div><span class="ok">→</span> Work Studio · http://127.0.0.1:4719</div>
						<div><span class="prompt">❯</span> <span class="cur"></span></div>
					</div>
				</div>
				<div class="term-status">
					<span><span class="st-ok">●</span> shell: bash</span><span>session: linxira</span><span>zetacode 1.4</span>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="block" id="ade">
		<div class="container">
			<div class="section-head">
				<div class="num">00</div>
				<div>
					<h2>An ADE, not an IDE</h2>
					<div class="sub">Zeta is an <b>ADE</b> (Agentic Development Environment). A classic IDE is built around a human writing code — editor, completion, debugger. An ADE is built around <b>agents doing the work</b>: the workbench is the agent's surface, and you set goals, review, and take over when it matters.</div>
				</div>
			</div>
			<div class="grid-table">
				<div class="grid-item"><div class="idx">01</div><h3>Agent-first</h3><p><code>zetacode</code> reads and writes files, runs commands and tests, and spawns sub-agents — not a chat box bolted onto an IDE.</p></div>
				<div class="grid-item"><div class="idx">02</div><h3>You still get an IDE</h3><p>When you want to edit by hand, open <code>zetaide</code>: editor, LSP and project views, with its own PTY terminals. It is also an ACP client, so external agents can run as its panel backend.</p></div>
				<div class="grid-item"><div class="idx">03</div><h3>One workbench, two editions</h3><p><strong>Work Studio</strong> is served by <code>zetacode serve</code> in your browser, or ships as a self-contained desktop app with a bundled Chromium. Same runtime, same sessions, nothing in the cloud.</p></div>
				<div class="grid-item"><div class="idx">04</div><h3>Three entries, one runtime</h3><p><code>zetacode</code>, <code>zetaide</code> and Work Studio all read the same persistent session store and speak the same host contract; coding and editing are handed off, not re-implemented per surface.</p></div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="block" id="suite">
		<div class="container">
			<div class="section-head">
				<div class="num">01</div>
				<div>
					<h2>One suite, three entries</h2>
					<div class="sub">Every entry launches standalone and ships its own <code>--help</code>; Work Studio is the workbench the agent and the IDE feed.</div>
				</div>
			</div>
			<div class="suite">
				<a class="suite-card hero-card" href="#install">
					<span class="pkg">@linxiraos/zeta</span>
					<div><span class="cmd">zetacode</span><span class="alias">agent CLI + TUI</span></div>
					<p>The coding agent: subagents, plan mode, LSP/DAP, hindsight memory, hashline editing, and time-travel rules out of the box. <code>zetacode serve</code> hosts Work Studio.</p>
					<div class="foot">Ships for <b>Linux x64</b> and <b>Windows x64</b></div>
				</a>
				<a class="suite-card" href="#install">
					<span class="pkg">@linxiraos/ide</span>
					<div><span class="cmd">zetaide</span><span class="alias">terminal IDE · ACP client</span></div>
					<p>TermIDE, vendored: its own PTY terminals, editor, LSP and project views. As an ACP client it starts external agents and renders their sessions in a panel, routing their permission, filesystem and terminal requests to the IDE.</p>
					<div class="foot">Runs <b>zetacode</b> inside a terminal pane too</div>
				</a>
				<a class="suite-card" href="#install">
					<span class="pkg">desktop · web-app</span>
					<div><span class="cmd">Work Studio</span><span class="alias">browser workbench · desktop app</span></div>
					<p>Session history, a PTY terminal panel, diffs, artifacts and media, stats, the plugin registry, the usage dashboard and the agent hub — one page, served by <code>zetacode serve</code>.</p>
					<div class="foot">Electron and browser editions are both <b>self-contained</b>: no Bun or Node needed</div>
				</a>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="block" id="workspace">
		<div class="container">
			<div class="section-head">
				<div class="num">02</div>
				<div>
					<h2>Work Studio, the workbench</h2>
					<div class="sub">One page served by <code>zetacode serve</code>: the renderer reads the same runtime, settings and <code>/api/v1</code> host contract as the CLI.</div>
				</div>
			</div>
			<div class="grid-table">
				<div class="grid-item"><div class="idx">01</div><h3>Session history</h3><p>Every session is kept and browsable; resume any branch from the workbench instead of scrolling a terminal buffer.</p></div>
				<div class="grid-item"><div class="idx">02</div><h3>A real PTY terminal panel</h3><p>Actual pseudo-terminals, not a log viewer: run <code>zetacode</code>, <code>zetaide</code>, tests, git, or any shell command beside the conversation.</p></div>
				<div class="grid-item"><div class="idx">03</div><h3>Diffs, artifacts, media</h3><p>Every change is rendered as a diff; generated files, images and other artifacts stay attached to the session that produced them.</p></div>
				<div class="grid-item"><div class="idx">04</div><h3>Stats and usage</h3><p>A local stats dashboard and usage view: sessions, tokens, and cost — computed on your machine, no telemetry account.</p></div>
				<div class="grid-item"><div class="idx">05</div><h3>Plugin registry</h3><p>First-party plugins ship in-tree and community plugins are listed in the registry; behaviour plugins work the same in every client.</p></div>
				<div class="grid-item"><div class="idx">06</div><h3>Agent hub</h3><p>Subagents and their sessions in one place: follow a running agent, read a finished one, and keep the main session clean.</p></div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="block" id="desktop">
		<div class="container">
			<div class="section-head">
				<div class="num">03</div>
				<div>
					<h2>Work Studio, on the desktop</h2>
					<div class="sub">Two editions from one staged runtime — the compiled <code>zeta</code> kernel, its native addons, and the <code>web-app</code> renderer. Neither needs Bun or Node installed.</div>
				</div>
			</div>
			<div class="grid-table">
				<div class="grid-item"><div class="idx">01</div><h3>Electron edition</h3><p>A desktop window with a bundled Chromium: the app starts the local service and hosts Work Studio itself, with no terminal and no system browser involved.</p></div>
				<div class="grid-item"><div class="idx">02</div><h3>Browser edition</h3><p>No bundled engine: the launcher starts <code>serve</code> and opens Work Studio in your default browser. The archive is zip on Windows and tar.gz on Linux.</p></div>
				<div class="grid-item"><div class="idx">03</div><h3>Windows builds</h3><p>An assisted per-user NSIS installer, plus a portable zip. Windows x64 is supported; there is no macOS build.</p></div>
				<div class="grid-item"><div class="idx">04</div><h3>Linux builds</h3><p>AppImage, <code>.deb</code> and <code>tar.gz</code>. Linux is the primary platform, and the same release also carries the CLI.</p></div>
			</div>
			<p class="install-note" style="margin-top: 1.4rem;">Desktop editions are published on <a href="https://github.com/Linxira-OS/linxira-zeta/releases/latest" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub Releases</a>. Install one and launch it; there is nothing else to install first.</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="block" id="terminal">
		<div class="container">
			<div class="section-head">
				<div class="num">04</div>
				<div>
					<h2>The terminal is still first-class</h2>
					<div class="sub">The workbench is a page; the place the agent actually works is still your shell.</div>
				</div>
			</div>
			<div class="grid-table">
				<div class="grid-item"><div class="idx">01</div><h3>zetacode in the TUI</h3><p>The full interactive agent: tool cards, previews, plan review, subagents — the same session Work Studio shows in the browser.</p></div>
				<div class="grid-item"><div class="idx">02</div><h3>zetaide as the terminal IDE</h3><p>TermIDE's panels and terminals, with an editor and LSP: a near-graphical engineering experience without leaving the terminal or an SSH session.</p></div>
				<div class="grid-item"><div class="idx">03</div><h3>External agents over ACP</h3><p>zetaide speaks the Agent Client Protocol as a client: agents such as Claude Code, through their adapters, run as a panel backend while the IDE owns permissions, filesystem access and terminal requests.</p></div>
				<div class="grid-item"><div class="idx">04</div><h3>One inside the other</h3><p>Run <code>zetacode</code> inside a zetaide terminal pane, or open Work Studio when you want the wide view. Same session, whichever surface you pick.</p></div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="block" id="install">
		<div class="container">
			<div class="section-head">
				<div class="num">05</div>
				<div>
					<h2>One command to the workbench</h2>
					<div class="sub">Install the CLI, then serve Work Studio — or install a desktop edition and skip the setup.</div>
				</div>
			</div>
			<div class="install">
				<div class="tabs">
					<button class="tab active" type="button" data-tab="npm">npm</button>
					<button class="tab" type="button" data-tab="bun">bun</button>
					<button class="tab" type="button" data-tab="linux">Linux</button>
					<button class="tab" type="button" data-tab="windows">Windows</button>
					<span class="tab-ind" id="tabInd" aria-hidden="true"></span>
				</div>
				<div class="code" data-pane="npm">
					<span class="cmd"><span class="prompt">$</span><span class="type" data-typing="npm install -g @linxiraos/zeta">npm install -g @linxiraos/zeta</span></span>
					<button class="copy-btn" type="button" data-copy="npm install -g @linxiraos/zeta" data-done="Copied">Copy</button>
				</div>
				<div class="code" data-pane="bun" hidden>
					<span class="cmd"><span class="prompt">$</span><span class="type" data-typing="bun add -g @linxiraos/zeta">bun add -g @linxiraos/zeta</span></span>
					<button class="copy-btn" type="button" data-copy="bun add -g @linxiraos/zeta" data-done="Copied">Copy</button>
				</div>
				<div class="code" data-pane="linux" hidden>
					<span class="cmd"><span class="prompt">$</span><span class="type" data-typing="curl -fsSL https://linxira-os.github.io/install.sh | sh">curl -fsSL https://linxira-os.github.io/install.sh | sh</span></span>
					<button class="copy-btn" type="button" data-copy="curl -fsSL https://linxira-os.github.io/install.sh | sh" data-done="Copied">Copy</button>
				</div>
				<div class="code" data-pane="windows" hidden>
					<span class="cmd"><span class="prompt">PS&gt;</span><span class="type" data-typing="irm https://linxira-os.github.io/install.ps1 | iex">irm https://linxira-os.github.io/install.ps1 | iex</span></span>
					<button class="copy-btn" type="button" data-copy="irm https://linxira-os.github.io/install.ps1 | iex" data-done="Copied">Copy</button>
				</div>
				<p class="install-note">
					Then: <code>zetacode serve</code> opens Work Studio in your browser.
					Terminal IDE: <code>npm install -g @linxiraos/ide</code> (<code>zetaide</code>).
					Slow mirror? Try <code>--registry=https://mirrors.cloud.tencent.com/npm/</code>.
					Updating: <code>zetacode update</code> self-updates the CLI; reinstall anytime with the script above.
					Desktop: Work Studio ships as an Electron app (Windows NSIS/zip; Linux AppImage, <code>.deb</code>, <code>tar.gz</code>) and a browser edition (zip/tar.gz) on
					<a href="https://github.com/Linxira-OS/linxira-zeta/releases/latest" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub Releases</a>.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="block" id="rooms">
		<div class="container">
			<div class="section-head">
				<div class="num">06</div>
				<div>
					<h2>One page, not one terminal per repo</h2>
					<div class="sub">Work Studio exists so you do not need a terminal tab per repository or branch. That is the goal, and the workbench already covers the part of it that ships.</div>
				</div>
			</div>
			<div class="grid-table">
				<div class="grid-item"><div class="idx">01</div><h3>Sessions, not tabs</h3><p>Every repository and branch gets a session in one workbench: start, resume, and switch without juggling windows.</p></div>
				<div class="grid-item"><div class="idx">02</div><h3>Agent hub</h3><p>Ships today: running and finished subagents in a single hub, with their own sessions and follow-up chat.</p></div>
				<div class="grid-item"><div class="idx">03</div><h3>Cross-process mesh</h3><p>Ships today: separate Zeta processes on one machine can find each other over the mesh, so sessions are not trapped in one terminal.</p></div>
				<div class="grid-item"><div class="idx">04</div><h3>Super-agent and rooms <span class="tag">experimental</span></h3><p>The direction is one agent per repository or branch, subagents, and cross-repo collaboration in shared rooms. This is <b>not implemented</b> yet — treat it as the roadmap, not a feature.</p></div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="block" id="agent">
		<div class="container">
			<div class="section-head">
				<div class="num">07</div>
				<div>
					<h2>A coding agent, out of the box</h2>
					<div class="sub">zetacode is built on the OMP runtime: core capabilities are built-in defaults, not optional extensions.</div>
				</div>
			</div>
			<div class="grid-table">
				<div class="grid-item"><div class="idx">01</div><h3>The full tool surface</h3><p>File read/write, search, patching, shell, git, task delegation, and structured tool output — heavy work runs on native Rust primitives.</p></div>
				<div class="grid-item"><div class="idx">02</div><h3>Sessions are trees</h3><p>Branching history: resume from any node. Terminal, browser, and desktop read one persistent session.</p></div>
				<div class="grid-item"><div class="idx">03</div><h3>Project tracking</h3><p>Turn on <span class="keycap">/tracking</span> and the agent writes milestones and key decisions to tracking docs — kept across sessions, browsable in Work Studio.</p></div>
				<div class="grid-item"><div class="idx">04</div><h3>Extensible to the bone</h3><p>TypeScript custom commands, a command marketplace, skills, MCP, full TUI hooks — the agent can customize itself on the spot.</p></div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="block" id="modes">
		<div class="container">
			<div class="section-head">
				<div class="num">08</div>
				<div>
					<h2>Four run modes</h2>
					<div class="sub">One agent, many interfaces — from the full TUI to scripts, event streams, and programmatic embedding.</div>
				</div>
			</div>
			<div class="modes">
				<div class="mode"><div class="mname"><span class="dot"></span>Interactive</div><p>The full TUI: panels, previews, tool cards.</p></div>
				<div class="mode"><div class="mname"><span class="dot"></span>Print / JSON</div><p><code>zetacode -p "query"</code> prints and exits; <code>--mode json</code> streams events.</p></div>
				<div class="mode"><div class="mname"><span class="dot alt"></span>RPC</div><p>A JSON protocol over stdin/stdout for non-Node integrations.</p></div>
				<div class="mode"><div class="mname"><span class="dot alt"></span>SDK</div><p>Embed the agent as a library and own its lifecycle.</p></div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="block" id="providers">
		<div class="container">
			<div class="section-head">
				<div class="num">09</div>
				<div>
					<h2>15+ providers, hundreds of models</h2>
					<div class="sub">Switch anytime in-session with <span class="keycap">/model</span>.</div>
				</div>
			</div>
			<div class="tag-row">
				<span class="tag hot">Anthropic</span><span class="tag hot">OpenAI</span><span class="tag hot">Google</span>
				<span class="tag">Azure</span><span class="tag">Bedrock</span><span class="tag">Mistral</span>
				<span class="tag">Groq</span><span class="tag">Cerebras</span><span class="tag">xAI</span>
				<span class="tag">Hugging Face</span><span class="tag">Kimi</span><span class="tag">MiniMax</span>
				<span class="tag">NVIDIA</span><span class="tag">OpenRouter</span><span class="tag">Ollama</span>
				<span class="tag">More…</span>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="block" id="bots">
		<div class="container">
			<div class="section-head">
				<div class="num">10</div>
				<div>
					<h2>Drive Zeta from chat apps</h2>
					<div class="sub">Plug zetacode into WeChat, Feishu, and Telegram — ask, dispatch tasks, and receive results where you already talk.</div>
				</div>
			</div>
			<div class="tag-row">
				<span class="tag hot">WeChat</span><span class="tag hot">Feishu</span><span class="tag hot">Telegram</span>
				<span class="tag">One bot interface</span><span class="tag">More platforms coming…</span>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="block" id="linux">
		<div class="container">
			<div class="section-head">
				<div class="num">11</div>
				<div>
					<h2>Native on Linux, not wrapped</h2>
					<div class="sub">Linux is the primary platform (Linxira OS, Arch-based); heavy work runs on native engines, and terminal and desktop are deeply integrated.</div>
				</div>
			</div>
			<div class="grid-table">
				<div class="grid-item"><div class="idx">01</div><h3>Native engines</h3><p>Text, regex, grep, and diff go to Rust primitives; the desktop editions stage the native addons alongside the compiled kernel.</p></div>
				<div class="grid-item"><div class="idx">02</div><h3>The terminal is home</h3><p>OSC 8 hyperlinks, native PTY management; the whole suite is terminal-born and works over SSH.</p></div>
				<div class="grid-item"><div class="idx">03</div><h3>Desktop bridge</h3><p>Open local editors and file managers safely from Work Studio: HMAC gateway tokens, target allowlists, workspace containment.</p></div>
				<div class="grid-item"><div class="idx">04</div><h3>One command to land</h3><p>npm / Bun global install, or <code class="keycap">curl … install.sh | sh</code>; Work Studio also ships as AppImage, <code>.deb</code>, and <code>tar.gz</code>.</p></div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="block" id="identity">
		<div class="container">
			<div class="section-head">
				<div class="num">12</div>
				<div>
					<h2>A first-class citizen of Linxira OS</h2>
					<div class="sub">Zeta is built by the Linxira OS team — a Linux distribution for research workstations. On Linxira OS, Zeta is not just another package.</div>
				</div>
			</div>
			<div class="grid-table">
				<div class="grid-item"><div class="idx">01</div><h3>First-class identity</h3><p>Zeta will run under its own system identity (a dedicated UID) as a system-level citizen — not a process parasitic on some user session.</p></div>
				<div class="grid-item"><div class="idx">02</div><h3>OS-enforced permissions</h3><p>Permission boundaries are enforced by the OS: Zeta declares policy, the system enforces it, changes need human approval — overreach is refused at the system layer.</p></div>
				<div class="grid-item"><div class="idx">03</div><h3>Private by default</h3><p>No access to personal data by default: private home files and other users' data are invisible to Zeta; every action is logged and auditable.</p></div>
			</div>
			<div class="cta-row" style="margin-top: 1.8rem;">
				<a class="cta ghost" href="/en/">About Linxira OS →</a>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>
</ZetaLayoutV2>
